# This subtree is version-controlled in the parent repo, which is public. It was
# previously excluded wholesale by the parent's `*/adcp/` rule, so everything
# below became publishable the moment that rule was removed.
#
# Eight per-project .gitignore files already exist further down (buyer, seller,
# governance, each agentcore/ and cdk/ dir). They cover their own subtree only.
# This file is the root-level net for artifacts that appear in more than one of
# them, or outside all of them.
#
# Each entry is either machine-local state or an artifact that a deploy step
# renders. Classification follows this project's own tooling — see
# render_aws_config.py, whose module docstring states which files are generated
# and which are committed.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored here: the seller's vendored session_records.py and
# seller_session_recorder.py. The canonical copies live in the buyer package and
# are committed; deploy_all.py's step_vendor_session_module copies them into each
# seller before it deploys, and the seller's own .gitignore ignores its copy.
#
# Verify with: python3 check_no_hardcoded_identity.py

# ── Secrets ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No real .env exists in the tree today; this is prevention, not cleanup. The
# .env.example files ARE committed and use the reserved 123456789012 account.
.env
.env.local
*.env
!.env.example

# ── Machine-local state ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Maps an instance prefix to the account+region that deployed it. The README
# describes this as local and gitignored; it holds a real account number.
deployments.local.json

# AgentCore CLI state for a specific deployment, including runtime ids. The whole
# directory rather than deployed-state.json and logs/ by name: only the seller's
# agentcore/.gitignore has a local `.cli/*`, so for governance this net was the
# only rule, and it had to enumerate every file the CLI happens to write.
**/.cli/

# ── Deploy logs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full deploy transcripts: real ARNs, account ids, runtime ids.
*.log

# ── Test caches ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pytest writes .pytest_cache/.gitignore containing `*`, so these directories have
# been ignoring themselves. Naming them here does not depend on that file
# surviving a clean, and three of them exist (buyer, seller app, governance app).
.pytest_cache/

# ── Rendered deploy artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The account+region the agentcore CLI deploys to. render_aws_config.py has a
# section headed "Why aws-targets.json cannot simply be committed".
**/agentcore/aws-targets.json

# Assembled by render_agentcore_json.py from agents/_shared/agentcore.base.json
# plus each agent's agentcore.fragment.json (deploy_all.py step 0.2,
# materialize-agentcore), then filled in place by the governance-origin,
# buyer-ui-origin and propagate-cognito steps. render_agentcore_json.py's
# docstring and deploy_all.py's step 0.2 comment both already describe this file
# as gitignored; until this rule existed it was not, and the two on-disk copies
# carried a CloudFront distribution id, two distribution domains and a Cognito
# user pool id. The fragments and the base stay tracked — the glob does not match
# their names.
**/agentcore/agentcore.json

# IAM policy documents rendered from their committed *-policy.template.json
# sibling, with the account substituted in. The templates stay tracked — the
# negation below is load-bearing, since the glob above would otherwise swallow
# them and a deploy would have nothing to render from.
**/app/*/*-policy.json
!**/app/*/*-policy.template.json

# ── OS / editor residue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
